--- body ---
## Context

Upstream flattens every newline before it cuts
(`chunker.ts:8`, `text.replace(/\s+/g, ' ')`, unchanged from the reviewed
`b132f2d` through v1.12.0), so the paragraph breaks the cache carries are gone
before the first boundary decision. That much is settled, and
`spec/DECISIONS.md` (2026-08-30) already ruled the chunk is the paragraph.

What is NOT settled is whether fixing it retrieves better, and the survey done
2026-08-31 says the field does not know either:

- Semantic chunking's gains are inconsistent and rarely justify their cost
  (Qu, Bao & Tu, NAACL Findings 2025), and with good embeddings the choice
  matters less than expected.
- ZotSeek's own ablation — the nearest neighbour in `verification/FIELD-REVIEW.md`,
  same corpus type — puts MRR 0,2514 at 512-token chunks against 0,2550 at
  2 000, and concludes size barely matters while the per-item chunk ceiling
  does. That gap is almost certainly noise at their query count, which is the
  point: they had the power to see it was noise.
- Against that, the largest cross-domain study (36 methods, 6 domains, 5
  embedders) puts paragraph grouping top and fixed-size CHARACTER chunking
  bottom. Upstream is in the second bucket, which is the sharpest thing that
  can be said for the fix without measuring it.

So the direction is plausible, the magnitude is unknown, and nothing in this
repo measures it: X5 hand-scores 50 cut points for segmenter PRECISION, which a
chunker can pass while moving no ranking at all. The price of being wrong is not
small — every stored vector is invalidated (salvage needs byte-identical text),
so every user re-embeds.

## Why this waits

R1 is the drawer's lock. "Every item in the search perimeter MUST become
searchable without anyone asking for it" is not met on the reviewed baseline:
`spec/README.md` has R1 at *partial*, with the crawl paging the library instead
of working a priority order, and a text-less attachment not recorded as
done-with-a-reason (ticket 0019). Better chunking of a partial library is a
refinement of the wrong thing.

`Blocked-by` names 0019 and 0026 as the machine-readable proxy: 0019 is R1's
unmet no-text clause, 0026 is the harness that would show convergence actually
reaches every item. The true condition is R1 standing at delivered in
`spec/README.md`; reopen on that, not merely on those two closing.

## The method, so it is not re-derived

Four properties make the comparison sound. The first three are what a naive A/B
gets wrong.

1. **Judge at the item level.** The chunker changes how an item is represented,
   not what is relevant to a query. Chunk-level labels are geometry-dependent —
   they must be rebuilt per arm, and the rebuild encodes the arm. Label items;
   score each arm by best-chunk-per-item, which is what `query()` already does;
   one judgment file then scores every arm untouched.
   This rules out `bench/vec_task_recall.mjs` as the scorer: its relevant set is
   "other chunks of the same item" and its `--gap` exclusion is defined in terms
   of the chunker's own 150-character overlap, so pointed at a chunker A/B it
   measures the geometry twice. Its own header says why.
2. **Queries must be geometry-blind.** Never generate a query from a chunk — the
   generating geometry wins by construction, which kills the cheap
   questions-from-passages route. Citation sentences are the right source: a
   sentence in item A citing item B, marker stripped, relevant item B. The labels
   exist independently of any chunker, they are query-to-answer shaped rather
   than topical-similarity shaped, and they are free at scale. Known bias: a
   citing sentence often paraphrases the cited abstract, so hits can land on
   metadata passages the chunker never touches — run body-only as primary
   (`source = 'fulltext'`), metadata-included as secondary.
3. **Control chunks-per-item, or measure chunks-per-item.** An item scores as its
   best chunk, so more chunks is more draws at the maximum — a higher score with
   no better alignment. #6012's own `CHUNK_MIN_TOKENS` comment states the
   mechanism; ZotSeek found the per-item ceiling mattered more than size. Three
   arms: (A) current 1 200/150, (B) paragraph-aligned, (C) current retuned to
   match B's chunks-per-item. **If B beats A but B is level with C, the result is
   chunk count and the alignment story is dead.** Arm C is one parameter and it
   is what makes the answer mean anything. Report passages-per-item per arm.
   Carry a negative control too: a metadata-only query set the change must not
   move.
4. **Power and paired statistics.** 0029's ~40 pinned queries is a regression
   gate, not a comparator. Paired bootstrap CI over queries — the interval, never
   a point estimate — and the decision rule pre-registered in `spec/DESIGN.md` §3's
   form before the run. Everything else held fixed: embedder, pooling, normalize
   and input template resolved through `bench/registry`, same fusion, same k,
   same extraction; both arms re-embed, since vectors cannot cross geometries.

## Substrate

The author's library first, committing scores only — the pattern 0025's substrate
map already uses for private-substrate experiments — because citation pairs are
abundant there and available now. 0029's committable corpus is a soft dependency
for the public half only; making it a hard one blocks the measurement on the
expensive side.

## What arm B should be

Not the segmenter. The smallest change that could move the number is the unwrap:
collapse newlines inside a block, keep blank lines, never let a chunk straddle
one. That is one line plus a boundary check, needs no tokenizer and no
`Intl.Segmenter`, and 0028's log records why it is also the prerequisite for
adopting #6012's geometry at all — that chunker splits on lines, so it is
paragraph-aligned only on text whose newlines already mean paragraph breaks.
Measure the cheap arm before pricing the expensive one.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] a citation-pair query extractor, labels at the item level
- [ ] `bench/chunker_ab.mjs`: three arms, item-level nDCG@10 / MRR / recall@10,
      paired bootstrap CIs, passages-per-item reported per arm
- [ ] the decision rule pre-registered in `spec/DESIGN.md` §3 BEFORE the run
- [ ] arm C reported beside B, and the verdict stated against C rather than A
- [ ] the negative control run and reported
